A||||19||LZeitgeist||IT WAS THE FREAKIN' BLEED SCREW!!!!!!||||1||
Z||000000||LZeitgeist||12-23-2001||09:55 AM||||<B>It was the ratzin' fratzin' fraggin' fleepin' bleed screw that was my problem!!</B><P>O.K. - Dad and I got a fresh start on my car ('88 Formula, new Archie clutch, no pressure to the slave cylinder, getting air in somewhere with every stroke of the pedal, etc.) Saturday morning... we pieced together the new slave cylinder, the '88 clutch line and the new master cylinder, all outside of the car, just to try and get some inspiration and new ideas... after we got them together, I asked Dad what he suggested as the best way to fill everything up, since they were all off the car and easy to get to everything. <P>We used the method he used to use when working on the brakes on antique private aircraft with a friend of his: we got one of those metal oilcans with the pump lever, connected a clear, flexable plastic line, and filled it with brake fluid. We got all the air out of the clear line, attached the line to the bleed screw in the back, opened it up and filled the system from back to front (I poured some fluid in the reservior to view the air coming out in the front). Once that was full, we tinkered around with it a bit, as I said, searching for inspiration - anything that hadn't already been thought of or tried in the previous week. <P>I showed him Rodney's technique of clearing the slave cylinder of air (by taking the bleed screw out completely), and that worked great... with what we'd just done, we felt as sure as we could be that there was no chance whatsoever that any air would be left in the system. So, we left that and went to the car.<P>Now the car currently has my original slave cylinder, my original master cylinder, and the hydraulic line left over from an '87 GT that we had swapped in for testing purposes. We loosened the slave cylinder mounting nuts all the way back to the ends of the bolts to give us room to relieve the pressure on the rod, and to also give us room to move the rod through its full stroke by hand as we were able to do with the parts we put together outside the car. We doublechecked all the connections, got all the air out of the clear flexable line attached to the pump can, attached it to the bleed screw, opened the screw, and Dad slowly began pumping fluid through the system as I watched the front reservoir to see whether air was coming out or if it was all fluid filling up the front. We made sure the pump can never went dry or got air into the system by watching the clear line for airbubbles, and I syphoned some fluid out of the reservoir and back into an empty 12-oz. brake fluid container when it got too full. Using this method, we put about 6-oz of fluid all the way through the already-filled system, and during the entire operation, it appeared certain that there was no air at all between the bleed screw and the reservoir. <P>After closing the reservoir in the front, I moved to the back and helped Dad close the bleed screw and remove the clear line without allowing air in. Then, we began to use Rodney's technique for purging the slave cylinder. I loosened the bleed screw to remove it completely, and as I took it out and handed it to Dad, I noticed that the 'seat' of the bleed screw was *covered* with dried, caked-on gunk!!!!! (Add "Change out/replace your hydraulic fluid" to your regular preventive maintenance list!!) When we were closing the bleed screw to what felt tight and secure, there was still *just* enough room to allow air in past the hardened gunk that was preventing the bleed screw from closing tightly against the seat in the slave cylinder... now why this problem didn't exhibit itself before we had begun this most recent clutch replacement job, we have no idea, and why installing the new slave cylinder didn't solve this problem confuses us even more... all we can think of is that in our aggravation and desire to 'get the damn thing fixed' after struggling for two and a half *extra* hours trying to get the transmission lined back up, etc., our techniques were faulty and we were making obvious errors in proper and secure installation, or we failed to refill the entire system properly with the new slave cylinder and discounted its effect on the problem when we saw what we took to be the same results as we had seen before that point in the situation. (Add "Take your time and take whatever breaks are necessary - even a number of *days* if needed - to think clearly when doing vexing work on your car" to your maintenance list as well!!)<P>So, we removed the new bleed screw from the new slave cylinder, made sure the old bleed screw would fit the new slave cylinder properly (and therefore the reverse would be true as well), and after compressing the rod on the slave cylinder slowly to purge it completely, we installed the new bleed screw, tightening it securely. We then released the pressure on the slave cylinder rod, and I removed the top to the reservoir in the front while Dad tightened the nuts on the slave cylinder bracket to put it back where it belonged aganst the release arm. After getting the fluid level in the reservoir to the proper level and reinstalling the cap, we again double checked our connections and mentally walked back through our methods, and agreed that we didn't think we could have possibly missed anything this time through. <P>So I sat in the car, and with my heart in my throat, I depressed the clutch pedal. Dad said that the release arm moved... for the first time in a week! So we switched places, and he pushed the pedal as I watched, and sure enough, there was plenty of movement on the arm, so we reinstalled the intake tube, closed up the front and the decklid, and I tried it out across the yard and down the street... it worked... *finally*. <P>Archie's clutch is as smooth as a dream, and I'm surprised how *little* pedal pressure is required to operate a clutch with (so I'm told) twice the clamping power of the original stock clutch! It feels awesome, and I will recommend this clutch to anybody/*everybody* I know with a V6/Getrag Fiero. Expensive, yes, but well worth the piece of mind to *know* I have the best that money can buy inside an installation process I hope I will never, ever have to do again. <P>Thanks everybody for your input, ideas and assistance, and I hope that our mistakes/corrections will help some of you do the job right the first time, even if you're irritated with your car when trying to work on it (as we were). Man, it feels good to be back on the road again... now to go drive through holiday shopping stop-and-go traffic to break the clutch in.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show 2001||||reg||1
Z||000001||Kasdan||12-23-2001||10:11 AM||||Sounds great!  I wish i had a manual  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kassig2.gif">                  <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering, Rebuilt 2.8<BR>http://www.geocities.com/mtyler14/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||12-23-2001||11:21 AM||||Don't be surprised if now you wind up with TOO MUCH slave throw. I've had to put pedal stops on 2 of my cars so far. You want the pedal to stop just past the point of proper clutch operation, excess throw can do nasty things to your new clutch.<BR>Good Luck||||reg||1
Z||000003||LZeitgeist||12-23-2001||12:37 PM||||Thanks, Phil - I remember you mentioning this earlier, and I wanted to ask you what are the things to listen, feel and look out for to avoid problems and damage? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||12-23-2001||12:47 PM||||You don't feel or hear a thing wrong until the one shift when it quits working.||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||12-23-2001||12:48 PM||||The only good thing(if you can call a problem good)about what you've just gone through is the satisfaction level afterwards is comensurate with the frustration level while you have the problem!Congratulations on solving your trouble.||||reg||1
Z||000006||LZeitgeist||12-23-2001||01:08 PM||||Phil - um, that doesn't sound promising... <BR>What part of the clutch fails from being overextended? Do the ends of the release fingers grind into the disc hub and get broken off? Or...?  I'm not overly concerned about it, as I usually have a pretty light foot on the clutch anyway, but I'd just like to form a more cohesive mental pic of the whole situation... I don't want to see the inside of that bellhousing again for the next ten years.<P>hugh - normally I would agree with you about the frustration/satisfaction level, but right now I'm still sorta P.O.'d at the whole dang car for failing me when I needed some *serious* emotional relief from other RL problems, but as I get less paranoid that things aren't going to start leaking again by the end of the week, I'll be feeling better about it... thanks for the congrats.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||12-23-2001||03:20 PM||||On my McLeod a welded ring that positions the spring diaphragm let go and a sports car racer told me the constant overthrow of the diaphragm will cause it to take a set which can cause it to slip at high rpm(of course he is dealing with MUCH higher rpms). The stop is so simple to install why take the chance? My Formula with Archie's CenterForce only needs about 3 1/2" of pedal throw to work perfectly. The wife's 88 coupe with a Sach's clutch only needs about the same throw also(have a stop on that one too).<BR>Happy motoring||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||12-23-2001||03:34 PM||||That is great!  I admire your tenacity, LZeitgeist!  Thank you for sharing your story - I nearly <B>always</B> learn something new when I read someone's tales of troubleshooting.<P>Phil - I would be interested to know, when the pedal stops are adjusted to their  <BR>"optimum" positions, what is the measured total movement of the slave cylinder rod (from pedal fully released, to pedal fully depressed)?  If you find time to take a measurement, it would be much appreciated.  Of course, if you don't find the time, I understand that, too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I assume you've been referring to your beautiful yellow 3800SC/ 5-speed Getrag.||||reg||5
Z||000009||LZeitgeist||12-23-2001||10:54 PM||||Phil - oh! O.K., so it's not that something breaks in an instant, it's that the spring pressure wears out prematurely... I see where you're coming from now. I'll doublecheck with Archie to see what the stock V6/Getrag specs are as opposed to your 3800/Getrag specs, but I'd much rather be too cautious with this thing while it's new than be regretful later when it's already worn out... thanks for the tip.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>StuGood - thanks!! I don't know what percentage was tenacity and what percentage was desperation, but either way, I'm glad it's done...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I'm glad I could help you stave off and/or correct possible future problems...||||reg||1
Z||000010||Archie||12-23-2001||11:19 PM||||While Phil has all of my respect and friendship, I have installed more than 170 of these CForce clutches and have never used a pedal stop. <P>I've never seen or heard of a problem with overtravel in any CForce clutch in a Fiero.<P>Archie<P>------------------<BR>Just how fast do you want to go?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phil||12-24-2001||06:55 AM||||I don't doubt it for a minute Archie, I did break a McLeod once and the stop is free(almost). It doesn't hurt anything so - what the heck, also now I don't have to push the pedal in as far to activate the starter interlock.<BR>Hoppy Halidays||||reg||1
